schema: claim-evidence
object:
  title: 42% of institutional knowledge is unique to the individual
  type: claim-evidence
  claim: >-
    In a 2018 study of more than 1,000 US workers, Panopto and YouGov found
    that 42% of institutional knowledge is unique to the individual —
    acquired for their current role and shared by none of their coworkers —
    meaning colleagues cannot do 42% of a departing person's job.
  evidence: >-
    Cited to "Panopto: Inefficient Knowledge Sharing Costs Large Businesses
    $47 Million Per Year" (panopto.com/company/news/...), a 2018 Panopto/
    YouGov survey of 1,000+ US workers.
  source_lineage: src/content/docs/metadata-that-matters.md
  interpretation: >-
    A named, dated, sourced statistic from an identifiable study — reasonably
    strong evidence for the article's framing device, though it is a
    corporate-sponsored survey (Panopto sells knowledge-management
    software), which is worth noting as a potential source-interest bias
    even if the methodology (YouGov-run survey) is credible.
  uncertainty: >-
    Low-moderate — the figure is specifically sourced and dated, but this
    ingest pass did not independently verify the original survey
    methodology or sample beyond the article's citation; the sponsor
    (Panopto) has a commercial interest in the finding.
  maturity: raw
  public_use: source-linked-unreviewed
  ai_assisted: true
  provenance:
    origin: src/content/docs/metadata-that-matters.md
    transformation: quoted
    authorship: ai-assisted
